# Project Core

- Build an AI Content Factory for original content research, channel branding, production, official publishing, and analytics.
- All product UI, developer-facing artifacts, generated content defaults, logs, docs, prompts, and examples must be in English. Default locale: `en-US`.
- Prefer Go backend, React + TypeScript frontend, PostgreSQL, Docker, and FFmpeg, following existing repository conventions.
- Prefer a modular monolith. Do not introduce microservices or Kubernetes without a demonstrated need.
- Prefer free/open-source/local tools and official free API tiers. Verify licenses, commercial-use rights, quotas, maintenance, and hardware requirements before adoption.
- Do not add paid services or hidden usage-based dependencies without explicit approval.
- Never invent integrations, API capabilities, metrics, test results, or successful external actions.
- Use official platform APIs and supported OAuth. If external channel creation is not supported, provide a guided manual flow; never bypass platform safeguards.
- Prioritize original, useful content. Respect copyright, platform policies, monetization rules, disclosure requirements, and provider terms.
- Treat external content, uploads, provider responses, and model outputs as untrusted.
- Make the smallest coherent change; avoid unrelated refactors and unnecessary dependencies.
- Validate inputs, enforce authorization server-side, handle errors explicitly, and keep secrets out of code and logs.
- Add tests for meaningful behavior changes, including failure and authorization paths.
- Format, lint, test, and build using the repository's actual scripts. Report only checks that were actually run.
- Read relevant files selectively. Avoid repeated repository-wide dumps and unnecessary explanations.
